What it is for
Example




&lt;h1&gt; to &lt;h6&gt;
Headings and subheadings, from most important (&lt;h1&gt;) to least important (&lt;h6&gt;). Each page should have, at most, one &lt;h1&gt;.
&lt;h1&gt;Page Title&lt;/h1&gt;


&lt;p&gt;
A paragraph of text.
&lt;p&gt;This is a paragraph.&lt;/p&gt;


&lt;a&gt;
A link (hyperlink) to another page or section, defined by the href attribute.
&lt;a href="https://www.w3.org"&gt;W3C&lt;/a&gt;


&lt;ul&gt;
An unordered list of items (with bullet points); each item goes inside an &lt;li&gt;.
&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;Item1&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Item2&lt;/li&gt;...&lt;/ul&gt;


&lt;ol&gt;
An ordered (numbered) list of items; like &lt;ul&gt;, items go inside &lt;li&gt;.
&lt;ol&gt;&lt;li&gt;First item&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Second item&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ol&gt;












Tag
What it is for
Example




&lt;div&gt;
A generic container, with no meaning of its own; used to group other elements (for instance, to apply a common style to them).
&lt;div class="card"&gt;...&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;header&gt;
The header of the page or of a section (typically title, logo, menu).
&lt;header&gt;&lt;h1&gt;My Site&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;/header&gt;


&lt;nav&gt;
A navigation block, typically a list of links.
&lt;nav&gt;&lt;a href="#home"&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/nav&gt;


&lt;main&gt;
The main content of the page; there is, at most, one per page.
&lt;main&gt;&lt;p&gt;Content...&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/main&gt;


&lt;section&gt;
A thematic content section, usually with its own heading.
&lt;section&gt;&lt;h2&gt;About&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;/section&gt;


&lt;aside&gt;
Related, but secondary, content (sidebar, notes).
&lt;aside&gt;&lt;p&gt;See also...&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/aside&gt;


&lt;footer&gt;
The footer of the page or of a section (copyright, contacts).
&lt;footer&gt;© 2026&lt;/footer&gt;


&lt;form&gt;
Used to build data-entry forms, together with input fields.
&lt;form&gt;&lt;input type="text"/&gt;&lt;/form&gt;









Reference for further study



This table covers only the tags used most often throughout this course. For the complete list of HTML elements, and the detailed specification of each, use the W3C (World Wide Web Consortium) reference, the international body that standardises HTML.

2.2 HTML Structure






A note on the code examples in this chapter



Several examples below are followed by a screenshot of the page as rendered in the browser. To keep every screenshot consistent with the code shown above it, the on-screen text in those specific examples (headings, menu labels, form fields) is left exactly as it appears in the original Portuguese screenshots, even in this English edition. Examples not tied to a screenshot use English text throughout.


A minimal HTML document always has the same shape: a document type declaration, followed by an html element, split into a head (metadata, not visible to the public, only to developers) and a body (the content visible to the public):
&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html lang="pt"&gt;
  &lt;head&gt;
    &lt;meta charset="UTF-8"&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;A Minha Página&lt;/title&gt;
  &lt;/head&gt;
  &lt;body&gt;
    &lt;h1&gt;Bem-vindo&lt;/h1&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Primeira versão da página.&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
This code is displayed in the browser as shown in Figure 2.1:






Figure 2.1: The minimal HTML page, as displayed in the browser: only the title and the paragraph from the body are visible.



Combining the structural tags already introduced, a page gains an organisation far clearer than a single block of undifferentiated divs. Each tag labels the kind of information it holds:
&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html lang="pt"&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
  &lt;meta charset="UTF-8"&gt;
  &lt;meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"&gt;
  &lt;title&gt;Página de Exemplo&lt;/title&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
  &lt;header&gt;
    &lt;h1&gt;Título do Site&lt;/h1&gt;
    &lt;!-- other elements in the header --&gt;
  &lt;/header&gt;
  &lt;nav&gt;&lt;!-- menu here --&gt;&lt;/nav&gt;
  &lt;main&gt;
    &lt;section&gt;
      &lt;h2&gt;Introdução&lt;/h2&gt;
      &lt;p&gt;Conteúdo inicial.&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;/section&gt;
    &lt;section&gt;
      &lt;h2&gt;Outra secção&lt;/h2&gt;
      &lt;p&gt;Podem colocar-se quantas secções se quiser.&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;/section&gt;
  &lt;/main&gt;
  &lt;footer&gt;© 2026&lt;/footer&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
This code is displayed in the browser as shown in Figure 2.2 — notice that, with no CSS at all, the structural tags carry no visual style of their own; the organisation into blocks exists, but is not yet visible:






Figure 2.2: The semantic page above, with no CSS applied: the header, the nav and the sections inside main organise the content, but are not visually distinguishable from one another.



Two elements are used with particular frequency in a Web application: a list of links inside nav, for navigation, and a form, to collect data from whoever uses the page.
&lt;nav&gt;
  &lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#home"&gt;Início&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#sobre"&gt;Sobre&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#contacto"&gt;Contacto&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/nav&gt;
This code is displayed in the browser as shown in Figure 2.3: with no CSS, a list of links still looks like a list, with bullet points and the browser’s default blue, underlined formatting for links:






Figure 2.3: The navigation list above, as displayed in the browser by default, with no CSS at all.



&lt;section&gt;
  &lt;h2&gt;Formulário de contacto&lt;/h2&gt;
  &lt;form&gt;
    &lt;label for="nome"&gt;Nome&lt;/label&gt;
    &lt;input id="nome" name="nome" type="text"&gt;
    &lt;label for="email"&gt;Email&lt;/label&gt;
    &lt;input id="email" name="email" type="email"&gt;
    &lt;button type="submit"&gt;Enviar&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;/form&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;
This code is displayed in the browser as shown in Figure 2.4: once again, with no CSS, the fields and the button appear with the browser’s default styling:






Figure 2.4: The contact form above, as displayed in the browser by default, with no CSS at all.



Note the for attribute of each label, which matches the id of the corresponding input: this is what visually links a label to its field, and what allows a screen reader to correctly announce the form. The input tag is one of the few tags that is not used with an opening and a closing pair: &lt;input&gt; &lt;/input&gt; — it only has the form &lt;input .../&gt;, whose trailing part means that this tag “opens and closes” itself. Most of the time, that trailing /&gt; is omitted and only &gt; is written, as shown in the code examples above.

2.3 Applying Styles Directly
HTML describes the structure of the page; the presentation is the responsibility of CSS (Cascading Style Sheets). This section shows the three ways of attaching CSS to HTML, and then the two main selectors — class and identifier — used to target those rules at specific elements.

2.3.1 Three Ways of Applying CSS
CSS can be attached to HTML in three distinct ways.
Inline, directly in an element’s style attribute. For example, on an h1 element:
&lt;h1 style="color:#0d6efd; margin:0.5rem 0;"&gt;Título do Site&lt;/h1&gt;
This code is displayed in the browser as shown in Figure 2.5:






Figure 2.5: The heading above, already coloured blue by the inline style.



Internal, in a &lt;style&gt; block inside the &lt;head&gt; of the html file it refers to:
&lt;head&gt;
  &lt;meta charset="UTF-8"&gt;
  &lt;style&gt;
    :root { --brand: #0d6efd; }
    body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; line-height:1.5; }
    nav ul { list-style:none; display:flex; gap:1rem; padding:0; }
    nav a { text-decoration:none; padding:0.4rem 0.6rem; }
    nav a:hover { background:var(--brand); }
  &lt;/style&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
The rule :root { --brand: #0d6efd; } defines a CSS variable (a custom property), reused later with var(--brand): it is useful for keeping the same colour in several places in the style sheet without repeating it, and for being able to change it in a single place.
External, in a separate .css file, linked through the &lt;link&gt; tag placed in the header of the document where the styles should apply:
&lt;head&gt;
  &lt;meta charset="UTF-8"&gt;
  &lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css"&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
/* styles.css */
:root { --brand: #0d6efd; }
body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; line-height:1.5; }
nav ul { list-style:none; display:flex; gap:1rem; padding:0; }
nav a { text-decoration:none; padding:0.4rem 0.6rem; }
nav a:hover { background: var(--brand); }
Applying these rules (through any of the three methods) to the navigation list shown earlier, the result is shown in Figure 2.6: the list’s bullet points disappear, the links stop being underlined, and they line up side by side:






Figure 2.6: The same navigation list, now with the CSS applied (list-style:none, display:flex, text-decoration:none).



Of the three, external CSS is, in general, the best practice: it clearly separates structure (HTML) from presentation (CSS), allows the same file to be reused across several pages, and keeps each document shorter and easier to read.


2.3.2 Classes, IDs and Specificity
Once the structure is defined in HTML, the CSS will hold a set of instructions telling the HTML elements how they should be “presented”. To apply styles to elements, CSS uses two main selectors: the class (class), for groups of elements, and the identifier (id), which must be unique within the page. A class can therefore label several elements on a page, which will all be formatted identically. Any number of elements can share the same class within the same document. An id selector, on the other hand, should only ever be applied to a single element on a page. The id is therefore said to be more specific than the class.
When writing CSS rules for a class, a dot is placed before the class name: .class_name, followed by the formatting instructions between { }, each separated by a semicolon. On the HTML side, the elements meant to be formatted by those rules carry the attribute class="class_name". For HTML elements with an id="id_name" attribute, formatting works the same way as for classes, except that instead of starting with ., the selector starts with # (#id_name).
A single HTML element can have several classes applied at once, separated by spaces. In that case, the element is formatted using the rules of ALL the applied classes:
  &lt;section class="noticias destaque com_hero"&gt; conteúdo da secção &lt;/section&gt;
Consider the following examples:
&lt;h1 id="titulo" class="titulo"&gt;Título do Site&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;nav class="menu"&gt;
  &lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a class="menu__link ativo" href="#home"&gt;Início&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a class="menu__link" href="#sobre"&gt;Sobre&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a class="menu__link" href="#contacto"&gt;Contacto&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/nav&gt;
:root { --brand: #0d6efd; }
#titulo { color: var(--brand); }              /* id: unique per page */
.menu { border-block: 1px solid #eee; padding: 0.75rem 0; }
.menu__link { text-decoration: none; padding: 0.4rem 0.6rem; }
.menu__link.ativo { font-weight: 600; }
This code is displayed in the browser as shown in Figure 2.7: the heading is coloured by the #titulo rule, and the menu is styled by the .menu/.menu__link rules, with the ativo link shown in bold:






Figure 2.7: The heading and menu above, now styled by the class and identifier rules.



When the same HTML element is styled by two conflicting CSS rules — for instance, both setting its colour — the rule that wins is determined by its specificity. The list of specificities, from highest to lowest, is:

!important (best avoided; makes the CSS hard to maintain)
Inline style, i.e., inside each element (style="...")
Identifier (#id)
Class (.class), attribute or pseudo-class
Element (h1, p, a, …)

For example, what colour would the heading from the previous example end up with, given this CSS?
h1 { color: black; }
.titulo { color: blue; }
#titulo { color: green; }  /* wins over the two rules above */
The identifier (#titulo) is more specific than both the class and the element, so it wins: the heading turns green, as shown in Figure 2.8:






Figure 2.8: The heading above in green: the #titulo rule wins over the h1 and .titulo rules, for being more specific.



Or, if !important is applied:
h1 { color: black !important; } /* wins because it has higher specificity */
.titulo { color: blue; }
#titulo { color: green; }
Now it is the element rule (h1) that wins, despite being the least specific of the three, because it carries !important: the heading turns black, as shown in Figure 2.9:






Figure 2.9: The heading above in black: the h1 rule wins over the .titulo and #titulo rules, despite being the least specific, because it carries !important.

2.4 Applying Styles Using Frameworks

2.4.1 Bootstrap
Writing and maintaining all of an application’s CSS from scratch is a considerable amount of work, especially when the result is expected to be responsive (adjusting to different screen sizes) and visually consistent. A CSS framework such as Bootstrap solves this problem, providing:

Productivity and visual consistency, through ready-to-use utility classes
Responsiveness already built in, through a grid and spacing classes
Tested, accessible components (forms, navigation bars, buttons, among others)

Bootstrap can be included directly via CDN (Content Delivery Network), with no installation required: a style sheet in the &lt;head&gt;, and a JavaScript file (for the interactive components) before the closing &lt;/body&gt;.
&lt;head&gt;
  &lt;link href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap@5.3.3/dist/css/bootstrap.min.css" rel="stylesheet"&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
  &lt;!-- page content --&gt;
  &lt;script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap@5.3.3/dist/js/bootstrap.bundle.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
A navigation bar (navbar), with Bootstrap, is ready in a handful of lines, already responsive (it collapses into a menu on narrow screens):
&lt;nav class="navbar navbar-expand-md bg-light border-bottom"&gt;
  &lt;div class="container"&gt;
    &lt;a class="navbar-brand" href="#"&gt;Meu Site&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;button class="navbar-toggler" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#nav1"&gt;
      &lt;span class="navbar-toggler-icon"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;div id="nav1" class="collapse navbar-collapse"&gt;
      &lt;ul class="navbar-nav ms-auto gap-2"&gt;
        &lt;li class="nav-item"&gt;&lt;a class="nav-link" href="#home"&gt;Início&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;li class="nav-item"&gt;&lt;a class="nav-link" href="#sobre"&gt;Sobre&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;li class="nav-item"&gt;&lt;a class="nav-link" href="#contacto"&gt;Contacto&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
      &lt;/ul&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/nav&gt;
This code is displayed in the browser as shown in Figure 2.10:






Figure 2.10: The navigation bar above, already styled by Bootstrap.



A form gets the same visual treatment through the form-label and form-control classes:
&lt;section class="container my-4"&gt;
  &lt;h2&gt;Formulário de contacto&lt;/h2&gt;
  &lt;form class="row g-3 col-lg-6"&gt;
    &lt;div class="col-12"&gt;
      &lt;label for="nome" class="form-label"&gt;Nome&lt;/label&gt;
      &lt;input id="nome" name="nome" type="text" class="form-control"&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;div class="col-12"&gt;
      &lt;label for="email" class="form-label"&gt;Email&lt;/label&gt;
      &lt;input id="email" name="email" type="email" class="form-control"&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;div class="col-12"&gt;
      &lt;button class="btn btn-primary" type="submit"&gt;Enviar&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/form&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;
This code is displayed in the browser as shown in Figure 2.11: compare it with Figure 2.4, the same form without Bootstrap:






Figure 2.11: The contact form above, already styled by Bootstrap (form-label, form-control, btn btn-primary).



The same grid system also organises the main content into columns. Bootstrap’s grid divides each row (row) into twelve columns; the class col-6 occupies six of those twelve, i.e., half the width:
&lt;main class="container my-4"&gt;
  &lt;div class="row"&gt;
    &lt;div class="col-6"&gt;
      &lt;p&gt;Conteúdo principal…&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;aside class="col-6"&gt;
      &lt;p&gt;Barra lateral…&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;/aside&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/main&gt;
This code is displayed in the browser as shown in Figure 2.12: the main content and the sidebar sit side by side, each occupying half the width, even though there is no visible border marking the columns — it is Bootstrap’s grid that reserves the space for each one:






Figure 2.12: The main content and the sidebar organised by Bootstrap’s grid.





2.4.2 Other Frameworks
Bootstrap is not the only CSS framework available, only the most beginner-friendly to start with, thanks to its approach based on ready-to-use components. It is worth knowing about, even without going into depth, a few popular alternatives:

Tailwind CSS

A utility-first framework: instead of ready-made components such as navbar or card, it provides low-level classes, one per CSS property (for example flex, pt-4, text-center), which are combined directly in the HTML to build any custom look.

Bulma

Based purely on CSS, with no JavaScript at all; syntax close to Bootstrap’s, but with no ready-made interactive components (menus, modals).

Materialize

Implements Google’s Material Design (shadows, elevation, vivid colours, animations); visually more opinionated than Bootstrap.

Foundation

Aimed at larger, more customised projects; more flexible than Bootstrap, but with a steeper learning curve.


The following table compares these frameworks by their approach and by ease of learning:








Framework
Approach
Learning curve




Bootstrap
Ready-made components (navbar, card, btn)
Low


Tailwind CSS
Utility classes, one per CSS property
Medium/high


Bulma
CSS components, no JavaScript
Low


Materialize
Components following Material Design
Medium


Foundation
Configurable, more flexible components
High

2.6 Exercise: the Static Pages of the “World Capitals” Application
Applying what was presented in the previous sections, this section builds the visual pages of the application used throughout the practical LABs (see Appendix D and onward): an API serving information about countries and their capitals. At this stage, the pages are entirely static, in HTML/CSS/Bootstrap, with no JavaScript at all: the data shown is fixed directly in the HTML, and the forms are not yet connected to any server. That is the work reserved for the LABs, which will simply add fetch() to this already-finished code.
All the pages share the same navigation bar:
&lt;nav class="navbar navbar-expand-md bg-light border-bottom"&gt;
  &lt;div class="container"&gt;
    &lt;a class="navbar-brand" href="index.html"&gt;World Capitals&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;button class="navbar-toggler" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#nav1"&gt;
      &lt;span class="navbar-toggler-icon"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;div id="nav1" class="collapse navbar-collapse"&gt;
      &lt;ul class="navbar-nav ms-auto gap-2"&gt;
        &lt;li class="nav-item"&gt;&lt;a class="nav-link" href="index.html"&gt;Países&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;li class="nav-item"&gt;&lt;a class="nav-link" href="capital.html"&gt;Consultar Capital&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;li class="nav-item"&gt;&lt;a class="nav-link" href="paises-editar.html"&gt;Gerir Países&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;li class="nav-item"&gt;&lt;a class="nav-link" href="login.html"&gt;Entrar&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
      &lt;/ul&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/nav&gt;
This code is displayed in the browser as shown in Figure 2.13:






Figure 2.13: The navigation bar shared by the four pages of the “World Capitals” application, already styled by Bootstrap.



From this shared navbar, four pages are built:

Country listing (index.html): a Bootstrap table, with a few countries fixed in the HTML as examples. In the LABs, this table becomes dynamically populated from the result of /country.
Authentication (login.html): a form with the fields user and pass, with the same names expected by the /login endpoint.
Capital lookup (capital.html): a simple form, with a single countrycode field.
Country management (paises-editar.html): three separate forms, one per operation, all pointing to the same /countryEdit endpoint, distinguished by the hidden operation field (see the note on this design in Chapter 7).

These four pages, linked to one another by the shared navigation bar, already form a small, complete, working Web site, even with no server-side logic at all: it is possible to navigate between them, fill in the forms, and submit them. What is still missing is a server to answer those requests, and that is what the material in the following chapters will provide.
The complete, self-contained code for the four pages, ready to copy into an editor and open directly in the browser, is gathered in Appendix A.

4.5 One Instance, Many Threads
The life cycle has an important consequence: there is a single instance of each Servlet in memory, even if N clients are using the same service at the same time. Each request is handled by a different thread, but all these threads share the same instance (Figure 4.4).






Figure 4.4: Several requests at the same time: the container handles each one in its own thread, but all of them invoke service() on the same single instance of the Servlet.









Beware of shared state



Never store data from a specific request in instance attributes of the Servlet: since the instance is shared by all threads, one request may read or overwrite data from another request in progress. Local variables, declared inside doGet() or doPost(), are safe, because each thread has its own copy.
WRONG: the name is kept in an attribute, shared by all requests
public class PessoaServlet extends HttpServlet {
    private String nome;                            // a single attribute for all threads

    protected void doGet(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) throws IOException {
        nome = request.getParameter("nome");        // 1 stores "joao"; 2, at the same time, "maria"
        response.getWriter().print("Olá, " + nome); // request 1 may answer "Olá, maria"
    }
}
RIGHT: the name is kept in a local variable, one copy per thread
    protected void doGet(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) throws IOException {
        String nome = request.getParameter("nome"); // each request has its own variable
        response.getWriter().print("Olá, " + nome); // each request answers with its own name
    }

4.6 From the HTTP Message to the Java Method
A Servlet is bound to a URL through the @WebServlet annotation, which maps a path to the class that will handle it:
@WebServlet("/pessoa")
public class PessoaServlet extends HttpServlet {
    // ...
}
A client request to http://localhost:8080/pessoa is thus routed to the PessoaServlet class. The container discovers this annotation when the application is installed (deployment). The same binding could be declared in the web.xml file, which is still found in older projects. From the elements of the HTTP message received (see Chapter 3), the container automatically builds the request object; the complementary response object is the one the Servlet uses to build the outgoing message. The elements of each message correspond to specific methods of these two objects (Figure 4.5):






Figure 4.5: The container converts the text of the HTTP request into an HttpServletRequest object and the HttpServletResponse object into the text of the response: the request line, the headers and the body of each message correspond to specific methods of these objects (the colours show the correspondence), and the Servlet only works with Java objects.



The following table summarises the most used methods:



HTTP element
Access in Java
When it is used




Request headers
request.getHeader(name)
Always, whatever the type of request


Parameters (query string of a GET, or application/x-www-form-urlencoded body of a POST)
request.getParameter(name)
Traditional HTML forms (see Chapter 2), in a presentation-oriented application


Raw request body (typically JSON)
request.getInputStream()
REST web services (see Chapter 7): the body is not in application/x-www-form-urlencoded format, so getParameter() does not interpret it, and the JSON has to be read and interpreted manually


Writing the response
response.getWriter() or response.getOutputStream()
Always, whatever the type of response



For example, for a GET /pessoa?nome=joao&idade=22 request:
protected void doGet(HttpServletRequest request,
                     HttpServletResponse response) throws IOException {
    String nome = request.getParameter("nome");        // "joao"
    String idadeTexto = request.getParameter("idade"); // "22": always arrives as a String
    if (nome == null || idadeTexto == null) {          // missing parameter: null
        response.getWriter().print("Faltam parâmetros");
        return;
    }
    int idade = Integer.parseInt(idadeTexto);          // 22: now it is a number
    response.getWriter().print(nome + " tem " + idade + " anos");
}
Two precautions follow from this example. First, getParameter() returns null when the parameter was not sent in the request, and that case has to be handled before the value is used. Second, it always returns a String, even if the value is a number: the age arrives as "22" and has to be converted with Integer.parseInt(...) before it can be used as a number.
The same data can arrive through an HTML form sent by POST:
&lt;form action="pessoa" method="post"&gt;
    &lt;input type="text"   name="nome"&gt;
    &lt;input type="number" name="idade"&gt;
    &lt;button type="submit"&gt;Enviar&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;
In this case, the browser sends the fields in the request body, in the format nome=joao&idade=22, and the name attribute of each field becomes the name of the parameter on the Servlet side. The request is handled by doPost(), but reading the parameters is exactly the same:
protected void doPost(HttpServletRequest request,
                      HttpServletResponse response) throws IOException {
    request.setCharacterEncoding("UTF-8");             // before reading the parameters
    String nome = request.getParameter("nome");        // the same method as in GET
    String idadeTexto = request.getParameter("idade");
    // ... the rest is the same as doGet()
}
The call to request.setCharacterEncoding("UTF-8") must be made before reading any parameter: without it, a name with accents, such as João, may arrive garbled at the Servlet. The request.getCharacterEncoding() method returns the encoding the request is using.
The getParameter() method returns a single value. When the same name appears several times in the request, as happens with a group of checkboxes or a multiple-choice list (cor=azul&cor=verde), getParameter("cor") does not fail, but returns only the first value, "azul". For these cases, the request object has three plural methods:

getParameterValues(name) returns a String[] with all the values of that name ({"azul", "verde"}), or null if the parameter does not exist;
getParameterNames() returns the names of all the parameters received, which is useful when it is not known in advance which fields come in the request;
getParameterMap() returns a map (Map&lt;String, String[]&gt;) from each name to all its values, to go through the parameters in one go.

String[] cores = request.getParameterValues("cor");   // {"azul", "verde"}
if (cores != null) {
    for (String cor : cores) {
        // handle each chosen colour
    }
}

4.7 Content-Type and the Shape of the Response
Before writing anything to the response, it is essential to set its Content-Type: this header tells the client how to interpret the bytes that follow, either as an HTML page or as JSON data. The order matters: setContentType() must be called before getWriter(), because that is the moment the writer fixes the character encoding, which is why it is worth stating charset=UTF-8, so that accented characters reach the client intact.
With the same data as in the previous examples (nome and idade), a presentation-oriented application answers with HTML, which the browser shows as a page (joao tem 22 anos):
response.setContentType("text/html; charset=UTF-8");
PrintWriter out = response.getWriter();
out.println("&lt;html&gt;&lt;body&gt;");
out.println("&lt;p&gt;" + nome + " tem " + idade + " anos&lt;/p&gt;");
out.println("&lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;");
A service-oriented application answers with JSON, which a client program receives as data ({"nome": "joao", "idade": 22}):
response.setContentType("application/json; charset=UTF-8");
PrintWriter out = response.getWriter();
String json = """
    {
      "nome": "%s",
      "idade": %d
    }
    """.formatted(nome, idade);

out.print(json);
It is the same data, for people (HTML) or for programs (JSON). Without a library dedicated to generating JSON, the most direct approach is to use a text block (delimited by """, available since Java 15), which avoids escaping the quotes, and the formatted() method, which replaces %s and %d with the values. Note that the age, being a number, goes into the JSON without quotes (%d). This construction is fine for simple values: a nome containing quotes would make the JSON invalid, so, for arbitrary data, a JSON library should be used.
To set the status code of the response (see Chapter 3), the HttpServletResponse class provides a constant for each code, avoiding “magic” numbers directly in the code:



HTTP code
Java constant




200
HttpServletResponse.SC_OK


201
HttpServletResponse.SC_CREATED


400
HttpServletResponse.SC_BAD_REQUEST


401
HttpServletResponse.SC_UNAUTHORIZED


403
HttpServletResponse.SC_FORBIDDEN


404
HttpServletResponse.SC_NOT_FOUND


500
HttpServletResponse.SC_INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR



Codes 401 and 403 differ in identity: 401 means the request is not authenticated (it is not known who it is), and 403 that it is authenticated but has no permission for this resource (it is known who it is, and they may not).
If the Servlet does not set any code, the container answers 200 OK. That is why the doGet() example shown earlier, when parameters are missing, is incomplete: it writes “Faltam parâmetros”, but the response tells the client that everything went well. A client error should carry the right code, 400 Bad Request, so that a client program detects it without having to interpret the text of the response:
if (nome == null || idadeTexto == null) {
    response.setStatus(HttpServletResponse.SC_BAD_REQUEST);  // 400
    response.getWriter().print("Faltam parâmetros");
    return;
}






What if the age is not a number?



If the request carries idade=abc, Integer.parseInt("abc") throws an exception (NumberFormatException). If the Servlet does not handle it, the container answers 500 Internal Server Error: the error was the client’s, but the response blames the server. The right approach is to catch the exception and also answer with 400 Bad Request:
int idade;
try {
    idade = Integer.parseInt(idadeTexto);
} catch (NumberFormatException e) {
    response.setStatus(HttpServletResponse.SC_BAD_REQUEST);  // 400
    response.getWriter().print("A idade tem de ser um número");
    return;
}

4.8 Where the Data Lives: the DAO
Tomcat has no Enterprise Beans: the business logic is implemented in ordinary Java classes. A Servlet that fetched the data itself (from a list in memory, from a database) would be tied to the source of that data.
In this chapter’s examples, the data comes from the request parameters or is fixed in the code, a deliberate simplification at this introductory stage. In a real application, it would come from one of those ordinary classes, dedicated to data access: a Data Access Object (DAO).
A DAO is not a formal Jakarta EE concept, nor does it require any special annotation or interface: it is just a common convention, a simple Java class that concentrates, in a single place, the way the data is obtained. It works as an abstraction layer that isolates the web layer from the data layer: the Servlet (web layer) only knows the DAO’s methods, and everything to do with how and where the data is stored stays on the other side. For a person’s data, it could look like this:
public class PessoaDAO {
    public Pessoa findByNome(String nome) { ... }  // list in memory, DB, file...
    public List&lt;Pessoa&gt; findAll() { ... }
}
The Servlet simply asks the DAO for the data, without knowing whether, behind those methods, there is a fixed list in memory, a database or any other source:
// in the Servlet: it does not know (nor need to know) where the data comes from
Pessoa p = dao.findByNome(nome);
The practical advantage is this: if the data moves, for example, from a list in memory to a database, only the DAO changes, and the Servlet stays exactly the same. In the MVC architecture, presented next, the DAO is part of the Model.

5.2 Cookies
A cookie is a key/value pair stored in the browser. When the server sends a cookie, it expects the client to send it back in all the following HTTP requests to the same domain, thus implementing an identification mechanism that works around the stateless nature of HTTP.
There are two types, depending on how long they last:

Session cookies: kept by the client only during a browsing session (setMaxAge(-1)). Until the session is closed, the client keeps identifying itself through the cookie. Once the session is closed (by logout or by closing the browser), the cookie is destroyed.
Permanent cookies: stored persistently, with a defined lifetime (setMaxAge(60*60*24*30): the time is expressed in seconds, in this case 30 days).

Creating a cookie:
Cookie cookie = new Cookie("tema", "escuro");
cookie.setMaxAge(60 * 60 * 24 * 30); // permanent cookie: 30 days
response.addCookie(cookie);          // the cookie is added to the response and sent to the client
Reading a cookie received in a request:
Cookie[] cookies = request.getCookies();
String tema = null;

if (cookies != null) {                       // null if the request carries no cookies
    for (Cookie c : cookies) {
        if (c.getName().equals("tema")) {
            tema = c.getValue();
        }
    }
}
The getCookies() method returns null, not an empty array, when the request carries no cookie, so the cookies != null check is mandatory.

5.4 Session-Based Authentication
Putting the two previous mechanisms together gives the classic session-based authentication pattern. After validating the credentials, the LoginServlet creates a session and stores the user’s identifier in it:
@WebServlet("/login")
public class LoginServlet extends HttpServlet {
    protected void doPost(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response)
            throws ServletException, IOException {
        String utilizador = request.getParameter("utilizador");
        String palavraPasse = request.getParameter("palavra-passe");

        if (dao.validarCredenciais(utilizador, palavraPasse)) {
            HttpSession session = request.getSession(true);
            session.setAttribute("utilizador", utilizador);

            response.setContentType("text/plain; charset=UTF-8");
            response.getWriter().print("Bem-vindo, " + utilizador);
        } else {
            response.setStatus(HttpServletResponse.SC_UNAUTHORIZED);
            response.setContentType("text/plain; charset=UTF-8");
            response.getWriter().print("Utilizador ou palavra-passe inválidos");
        }
    }
}
Note that dao is an instance of a Data Access Object (DAO), the object dedicated to data access already mentioned in Chapter 4 (for example, a UtilizadorDAO). In this case, it is responsible for validating the credentials received: it typically queries a user database and returns true or false depending on whether the user/password pair is valid.
Any protected endpoint checks, at the start of its own doGet() or doPost(), whether there is a valid session with that attribute:
HttpSession session = request.getSession(false);
if (session == null || session.getAttribute("utilizador") == null) {
    response.setStatus(HttpServletResponse.SC_UNAUTHORIZED);
    response.getWriter().print("Autenticação necessária");
    return;
}
// authenticated request: carry on with normal processing
Logging out simply means ending the session:
session.invalidate();

5.6 A Simple JSP Example
&lt;%@ page language="java" contentType="text/html; charset=UTF-8" pageEncoding="UTF-8"%&gt;

&lt;% String utilizador = (String) request.getAttribute("utilizador"); %&gt;

&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
  &lt;meta http-equiv="Content-Type" content="text/html; charset=UTF-8"&gt;
  &lt;title&gt;Uma Página JSP&lt;/title&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
  &lt;h1&gt;Olá, &lt;%= utilizador %&gt;!&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
The &lt;% ... %&gt; block (scriptlet) contains arbitrary Java code; the &lt;%= ... %&gt; block (expression) inserts the value of a Java expression directly into the generated HTML. It is precisely the utilizador attribute, placed in the request by the Servlet through request.setAttribute(...), that the JSP retrieves here.






Expression Language (EL)



Scriptlets are now considered old practice, because they mix Java code with HTML. In current JSPs, the same line is written with the Expression Language, which looks up the attribute by name in the various scopes (see Section 5.8), from the narrowest to the broadest:
&lt;h1&gt;Olá, ${utilizador}!&lt;/h1&gt;

5.8 Scopes: Sharing Information Inside the Server
Just as Servlets can share information with other modules through attributes (see Chapter 4), those attributes always exist within a given scope. There are four kinds of scope:

Web context (or application context): the data is seen by the whole application, in every request from every user (for example, the address of the database)
Session: the data belongs to one user and is kept across that user’s requests (for example, who logged in)
Request: the data exists only during one request and is lost as soon as the response is sent (for example, the data the Servlet passes to the JSP)
Page: exclusive to JSP, not used in this course

All scopes follow the same interface: setAttribute/getAttribute methods, and getAttributeNames() to list everything stored there.
Web context, shared by the whole application:
ServletContext context = request.getServletContext();
context.setAttribute("dbHost", "localhost");
String dbHost = (String) context.getAttribute("dbHost");
Session, already presented, shared only by the same user:
HttpSession session = request.getSession();
session.setAttribute("email", "foo@fool.com");
String email = (String) session.getAttribute("email");
Request, the narrowest of all, shared only between modules that process the same request (for example, between a Servlet and the JSP it forwards to):
request.setAttribute("email", "foo@fool.com");
String email = (String) request.getAttribute("email");
The four scopes form a containment hierarchy, from the broadest to the narrowest: Application contains Session, which contains Request, which contains Page. The larger the scope, the more widely the information is shared, and the greater the responsibility for managing it correctly (for example, removing it when it is no longer needed).






Figure 5.5: The four scopes, from the broadest (Application) to the narrowest (Page): each contains the next, and a larger scope means more sharing, but also more responsibility in managing it.









Scopes are not instantiated



The scope objects (ServletContext, HttpSession, ServletRequest) are provided automatically by the container. A new instance is never created with new: they are obtained from the request. The getServletContext() method always returns the same context, which exists while the application is running; the getSession() method returns the existing session or, if there is none yet, creates a new one.

6.2 Authentication vs. Authorisation
Before solving the problem, it is worth clarifying two concepts that are often confused:

Authentication

Answers “who is the user?”. It is the identity check, typically done at login time.


if (dao.validarCredenciais(utilizador, palavraPasse)) {
    HttpSession session = request.getSession(true);
    session.setAttribute("utilizador", utilizador);
}

Authorisation

Answers “what is this user allowed to access?”. It is the permission check, done after the identity has been confirmed.


if ("admin".equals(session.getAttribute("perfil"))) {
    // access allowed
}
In this example, the login stored in the session, besides the user, their profile (for example, "admin"), and only users with that profile have access to the resource.
Authentication always comes first: it makes no sense to check the permissions of someone whose identity has not yet been confirmed. This distinction is directly reflected in the HTTP status codes used in the response (see Chapter 3):

401 Unauthorized: the request is not authenticated
403 Forbidden: the request is authenticated, but has no permission for the requested resource

6.7 Declaring and Implementing a Filter
A Filter is declared with the @WebFilter annotation, indicating the URL pattern it applies to:
@WebFilter("/*")
public class AuthenticationFilter implements Filter {
    // ...
}
The "/*" pattern intercepts all requests, without exception. The class must implement jakarta.servlet.Filter, and the container discovers it automatically at deployment time. Like Servlets, a Filter can also be declared equivalently in the web.xml file, although this practice is no longer widely used.
A typical doFilter(), which protects all requests by requiring a valid session, looks like this:
public void doFilter(ServletRequest request,
                      ServletResponse response,
                      FilterChain chain)
        throws IOException, ServletException {

    HttpServletRequest req = (HttpServletRequest) request;
    HttpServletResponse res = (HttpServletResponse) response;

    HttpSession session = req.getSession(false);

    if (session == null || session.getAttribute("utilizador") == null) {
        res.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_UNAUTHORIZED); // 401
        return;
    }

    chain.doFilter(request, response);
}
In practice, not all requests should be protected: /login itself, and static resources such as /css, /js or /images, must remain accessible without authentication, otherwise the user could never even reach the login form. The complete version of doFilter() handles these exceptions first and only then checks the session:
public void doFilter(ServletRequest request,
                      ServletResponse response,
                      FilterChain chain)
        throws IOException, ServletException {

    HttpServletRequest req = (HttpServletRequest) request;
    HttpServletResponse res = (HttpServletResponse) response;

    String ctx = req.getContextPath();   // e.g. "/MinhaApp"
    String path = req.getRequestURI();   // e.g. "/MinhaApp/css/estilo.css"

    boolean isLogin = path.endsWith("/login");
    boolean isStatic = path.startsWith(ctx + "/css")
                     || path.startsWith(ctx + "/js")
                     || path.startsWith(ctx + "/images");

    if (isLogin || isStatic) {           // public resources
        chain.doFilter(request, response);
        return;
    }

    HttpSession session = req.getSession(false);

    if (session == null || session.getAttribute("utilizador") == null) {
        res.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_UNAUTHORIZED); // 401
        return;
    }

    chain.doFilter(request, response);
}
The getContextPath() method returns the prefix under which the application is installed on the server, and getRequestURI() returns the full requested path. Comparing the start of the path with ctx + "/css" works whatever the name under which the application was installed.
Everything a Filter does after chain.doFilter() returns happens with the response already produced by the Servlet. An activity logging Filter, for example, notes the time before passing the request to the chain and computes the duration when the chain returns:
@WebFilter("/*")
public class RegistoFilter implements Filter {

    public void doFilter(ServletRequest request,
                          ServletResponse response,
                          FilterChain chain)
            throws IOException, ServletException {

        long inicio = System.currentTimeMillis();   // before the Servlet

        chain.doFilter(request, response);          // the Servlet runs here

        long duracao = System.currentTimeMillis() - inicio;   // after
        HttpServletRequest req = (HttpServletRequest) request;
        System.out.println(req.getRequestURI() + ": " + duracao + " ms");
    }
}
The inicio and duracao variables are local: each request has its own, and concurrent requests do not interfere with one another, as recommended in the life cycle.

8.4 The Life Cycle of a Token
A token does not replace the authentication moment itself: it only replaces the way the server remembers it (Figure 8.4).

Issuing

The client authenticates exactly as in Chapter 5, sending utilizador and palavraPasse to a login endpoint. The server validates the credentials (for example, with dao.validarCredenciais(), see Chapter 4) but, instead of creating an HttpSession, generates a new JWT, signs it with its secret key and returns it to the client in the response body

Storing and resending

Unlike the JSESSIONID (see Chapter 5), which the browser sends back automatically through a cookie, the client is responsible for storing the token received and for resending it, explicitly, in the Authorization header of each following request. In a JavaScript client, the token is kept in a variable or in sessionStorage (a browser storage area that lasts while the tab is open). Any JavaScript on the page can read what is stored there, including malicious code injected into the page (see Section 8.7)

Validation

Each request is validated completely independently of the previous ones: the server recomputes the signature, without looking up any session (see Section 8.5)

Expiry

The exp claim of the payload defines until when the token is valid. After that moment, the server rejects it (401) even if the signature is still correct, forcing a new login. This limits the time window in which an intercepted token could be misused. Unlike an HttpSession, which only ends by explicit logout or prolonged inactivity (see Chapter 5), a JWT always expires, even if it keeps being used








Figure 8.4: The life cycle of a token: the login returns a JWT, which the client resends with each request; when the exp claim passes, the server answers 401 and the client has to log in again.



When issuing, the token is rarely built by hand. JJWT is an open-source Java library for creating and validating JWTs; its main class, Jwts, has a method for each of these tasks: Jwts.builder() creates a token and Jwts.parser() validates it. Generating a token boils down to indicating the user, the expiry and the key:
String token = Jwts.builder()
        .subject(utilizador)                  // "sub" claim
        .issuedAt(new Date())                 // "iat" claim (issued at): now
        .expiration(new Date(System.currentTimeMillis()
                + 30 * 60 * 1000))            // "exp": in 30 min (in ms)
        .signWith(SECRET_KEY, Jwts.SIG.HS256) // the signature
        .compact();                           // joins the three parts
The login Servlet then returns this string in the response body, for example inside a JSON such as {"token":"eyJhbGciOi..."}. The complete code, with the JwtUtil class, is in Appendix D5.
Note the units: Java counts time in milliseconds (System.currentTimeMillis(), new Date(...)), so 30 minutes are 30 * 60 * 1000; the exp claim, on the other hand, is counted in seconds. There is no need to convert by hand: JJWT converts the date to seconds when writing the claim.
The SECRET_KEY deserves care, because the whole security depends on it: whoever has it can forge tokens for any user. It should be generated once, at random, with at least 32 bytes (256 bits) for HS256 (for example, with Jwts.SIG.HS256.key().build() or openssl rand -base64 32), and stored outside the code, typically in an environment variable of the server:
SecretKey SECRET_KEY = Keys.hmacShaKeyFor(
        System.getenv("JWT_SECRET").getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
All servers use the same key (see Section 8.5), and it must never go into a Git repository or reach the client.
An environment variable is a name = value pair that the operating system keeps outside any program and hands to each program when it starts; the program reads the value by name, without that value being written in the code. Every computer already has several, such as PATH (the folders where the system looks for programs), HOME (the user’s folder) or JAVA_HOME (where Java is installed, used by Tomcat itself). For the JWT key, the variable is set on the server machine before starting the application:
export JWT_SECRET="k3J9...32-bytes-or-more-at-random..."   # macOS and Linux
set JWT_SECRET=k3J9...32-bytes-or-more-at-random...        :: Windows
In Tomcat, the usual place for these settings is the bin/setenv.sh file (or bin/setenv.bat, on Windows), which Tomcat runs every time it starts. In the Java code, the value is read with System.getenv("JWT_SECRET"), as in the excerpt above.
This solution has three advantages: the secret is not in the code, so whoever sees the code or the repository does not get it; it can be changed without recompiling the application, simply by changing the variable and restarting the server; and each machine can have its own value (the developer’s, the test one and the production one), with all production servers receiving the same one. It is not, however, a vault: the variable is visible to the programs on that machine and to whoever administers it. Larger systems use dedicated secret management services, which are outside the scope of this course.
The following table summarises the difference between the two authentication models seen in this course:









Session (HttpSession)
JWT




Where the state lives
On the server, in memory
In the token itself, on the client side


What the client keeps
An identifier (JSESSIONID)
The full token, already with the data


Validation on each request
Lookup in the server’s memory
Signature check, without looking anything up


Several servers
Need to share the session among them
Each one validates independently



In practice, a Filter (see Chapter 6) is still the natural place for this validation to happen. Recall the session-based authentication Filter, already presented in that chapter:
public void doFilter(ServletRequest request, ServletResponse response,
                     FilterChain chain) throws IOException, ServletException {

    HttpServletRequest req = (HttpServletRequest) request;
    HttpServletResponse res = (HttpServletResponse) response;

    HttpSession session = req.getSession(false);

    if (session == null || session.getAttribute("utilizador") == null) {
        res.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_UNAUTHORIZED); // 401
        return;
    }

    chain.doFilter(request, response);
}
The equivalent token-based version has exactly the same structure: only the check itself changes, from a lookup in the HttpSession to validating the signature and expiry of the token. Figure 8.5 shows both side by side.






Figure 8.5: Session Filter and JWT Filter: in both, a failed check ends the request with 401 and a successful check continues the chain with chain.doFilter(); the JWT Filter only has one extra step, because it first confirms that the header carries a Bearer token and then validates it.



That validation is rarely written from scratch: it is sensitive code, involving cryptography, and it pays to leave it to a tested and actively maintained library, rather than reimplementing it. JJWT, already used for issuing, is one of the most used in Java, and one of the most careful about security. For example, it protects against the "alg":"none" attack: the attacker changes the payload (for example, to "role":"admin"), writes "alg":"none" in the header and deletes the signature. A careless library would read the header, conclude that there is no signature to check and accept the forged token, giving the attacker the privileges they wrote themselves. JJWT rejects unsigned tokens whenever it is given a key with verifyWith():
public void doFilter(ServletRequest request, ServletResponse response,
                     FilterChain chain) throws IOException, ServletException {

    HttpServletRequest req = (HttpServletRequest) request;
    HttpServletResponse res = (HttpServletResponse) response;

    String authHeader = req.getHeader("Authorization");

    if (authHeader == null || !authHeader.startsWith("Bearer ")) {
        res.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_UNAUTHORIZED); // 401
        return;
    }

    // strip "Bearer " (7 characters): only the token is left
    String token = authHeader.substring("Bearer ".length());

    try {
        Claims claims = Jwts.parser()
                .verifyWith(SECRET_KEY)          // the server's secret key
                .build()
                .parseSignedClaims(token)
                .getPayload();

        req.setAttribute("utilizador", claims.getSubject());
    } catch (JwtException e) {
        // invalid signature or expired token
        res.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_UNAUTHORIZED);
        return;
    }

    chain.doFilter(request, response);
}
The call to parseSignedClaims(token) does, internally, everything the validation requires: it decodes the header and the payload, recomputes the signature with SECRET_KEY and compares it with the one received, and checks the exp claim; it throws a JwtException (or one of its subclasses, such as ExpiredJwtException for an expired token, or SignatureException for an invalid signature) if any of these checks fails. Note the structural similarity with the session-based Filter: in both cases, a failure condition ends the request with 401, and the chain only continues with chain.doFilter() if the authentication is valid.

8.7 Security Precautions with Tokens
A JWT is a “bearer” credential: whoever has it is treated as its owner until it expires. The signature prevents it from being forged, but not from being stolen, and most of the precautions are against theft.

Network interception

Without HTTPS, the request travels in the clear, and anyone on the same network (on a public Wi-Fi, for example) can read the Authorization header and copy the token. The defence is to always use HTTPS, without exceptions

XSS (Cross-Site Scripting)

The attacker manages to get their own JavaScript running on the page another person is viewing. The most common way is through data the application receives from one user and shows to others without treating it: a comment, a name, the title of a book. If the page inserts that text as HTML, the browser runs whatever code is hidden in it, with the same rights as the page itself. That code can, for example, read the token stored in sessionStorage and send it to the attacker, who can then impersonate the victim (Figure 8.8)








Figure 8.8: Stealing a token through XSS: the attacker posts a comment with hidden code (1); the server stores it and returns it to the victim (2); the page shows it as HTML and the browser runs the code, which reads the token (3) and sends it to the attacker (4); the attacker uses it in their own requests (5), and the server cannot tell them apart from the victim.



The example uses an image with an invalid address (&lt;img src=x onerror="..."&gt;) rather than a &lt;script&gt; tag: browsers do not run a &lt;script&gt; inserted with innerHTML, but they do run the code of the onerror attribute when the image fails to load. The defence is to always insert the data received as text, with textContent, and never as HTML, with innerHTML (see Section 8.10): the comment appears exactly as it was written and nothing is executed.
Besides these two risks, there is a set of good practices to follow whenever tokens are used:

Short expiry: the exp claim should be minutes or a few hours, to limit what a stolen token allows;
A JWT cannot be “switched off” before it expires: since the server stores nothing, logout merely deletes the token on the client side; to truly revoke it, a list of banned tokens would have to be kept, that is, state would have to return to the server (one more reason for a short expiry);
No sensitive data in the payload: anyone can read it, so it must never contain passwords, tax numbers or unnecessary personal data;
A long, random secret key, kept outside the code (see Section 8.4);
Enforce the algorithm when validating, with verifyWith(), against the "alg":"none" attack (see Section 8.4);
Always validate on the server, on every request, the signature and the expiry: that is the Filter’s job.

Appendix B goes deeper into these issues, in particular the choice between keeping the token in sessionStorage/localStorage or in an HttpOnly cookie (see Section B.6).

8.9 Consuming a REST API in JavaScript: fetch() and JSON
The Fetch API is the standard way for a browser to issue HTTP requests from JavaScript, without reloading the page. A simple request, without authentication, looks like this:
fetch("/livros/12")
  .then(response =&gt; response.json())
  .then(data =&gt; {
    console.log(data.titulo);
  });
fetch() returns a promise: a value that is not yet available, but will be later, when the request finishes. The first .then() receives the raw HTTP response and converts its body, in JSON text, into an ordinary JavaScript object through response.json(). This step also returns a promise, so it is chained with a second .then(), with the data ready to use.
For an authenticated request (see Section 8.2 and Section 8.3), the token travels in a header, like any other authenticated HTTP request:
fetch("/livros", {
  headers: { "Authorization": "Bearer " + token }
})
  .then(response =&gt; response.json())
  .then(data =&gt; {
    // ...
  });
A fetch() with an expired or invalid token does not fail: the request reaches the server and comes back with status 401. It is up to the client to check the status of the response before using the body, through response.ok (true for 2xx statuses), and to ask for a new login when the server rejects the token:
fetch("/livros", {
  headers: { "Authorization": "Bearer " + token }
})
  .then(response =&gt; {
    if (response.status === 401) {
      window.location.href = "/login.html";   // expired or invalid token: new login
      return;
    }
    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error("Erro " + response.status);
    }
    return response.json();
  })
  .then(livros =&gt; {
    // ... show the books
  });
A POST request, creating a resource, carries the body already in JSON, stating that format explicitly in the Content-Type header (see Chapter 4):
fetch("/livros", {
  method: "POST",
  headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
  body: JSON.stringify({ titulo: "Os Maias", autor: "Eça de Queirós" })
})
  .then(response =&gt; response.json())
  .then(data =&gt; {
    // ...
  });

8.10 The DOM as the Browser’s “View”
The DOM (Document Object Model) is the in-memory representation of the HTML page the browser is showing: a tree of objects, one for each element of the page, which JavaScript can read and change directly. It is through the DOM that JavaScript plays the role of View (see Section 8.8): it does not generate HTML as text, as a JSP does (see Chapter 5), but finds elements already present on the page and updates their content.
fetch("/livros/12")
  .then(response =&gt; response.json())
  .then(data =&gt; {
    document.querySelector("#titulo").textContent = data.titulo;
  });
document.querySelector(selector) finds the first element on the page matching the selector given (here, the element with id="titulo"), returning a reference to that DOM element; textContent is the property that sets the text shown inside it. The same principle applies to a list of results: for each item returned by the API, a new HTML element is created and inserted into the page.
fetch("/livros")
  .then(response =&gt; response.json())
  .then(livros =&gt; {
    const tabela = document.querySelector("#tabela-livros");
    livros.forEach(livro =&gt; {
      const linha = document.createElement("tr");
      for (const valor of [livro.id, livro.titulo, livro.autor]) {
        const celula = document.createElement("td");
        celula.textContent = valor;     // text, never HTML
        linha.appendChild(celula);
      }
      tabela.appendChild(linha);
    });
  });
Each cell is filled with textContent, not innerHTML. The difference matters: innerHTML interprets the value as HTML, and a book title coming from the server that contained, for example, &lt;img src=x onerror="..."&gt; would make the browser run that JavaScript on the page (a Cross-Site Scripting, or XSS, attack). With textContent, the value is always inserted as text, whatever its content.
This pattern, requesting data from a REST API with fetch() and using the DOM to show it, is the basis of any JavaScript front-office connected to a REST service, regardless of the specific application or the data involved.

