E.1.6 Step 2.6: Protect an Endpoint with Session Verification
With login working, the /country endpoint (the CountryServlet from Appendix D) is now protected, so that it only responds to requests from authenticated users.
It is worth distinguishing two concepts here (see Chapter 6): authentication — confirming who the user is — and authorisation — confirming whether that user has permission to access a given resource. This step is only about authentication: checking whether a valid session exists.
At the start of CountryServlet’s doGet method, add:
HttpSession session = request.getSession(false);

if (session == null || session.getAttribute("UserName") == null) {
    response.setStatus(HttpServletResponse.SC_UNAUTHORIZED);
    response.setContentType("application/json");
    PrintWriter out = response.getWriter();
    String jsonResponse = "{\"error\": \"Authentication required\"}";
    out.print(jsonResponse);
    return;
}
Note the use of request.getSession(false): unlike what happens at login (getSession(true)), here a new session should not be created just to check whether it exists — if there is no session, getSession(false) returns null. It is essential not to forget the return after writing the error response: without it, the code would keep executing the rest of the method, even after already having sent a 401 response.


E.1.7 How to Test

Access /country?countrycode=PT directly, without logging in first. Expected result: HTTP 401, with the body {"error": "Authentication required"}
Submit the login.html form with javaee / asw2026. Expected result: HTTP 200, with the body {"message": "OK", "user": "javaee"}
Repeat the request to /country?countrycode=PT. Expected result now: HTTP 200, with the country’s data

This LAB leaves the session-verification logic scattered across every Servlet that needs protection — in Appendix F, that logic is centralised in a single Filter.

F.1.1 Step 3.1: Create and Configure the Authentication Filter

Create a new class called AuthenticationFilter, in the capitaisapi.filters package
This class should implement the jakarta.servlet.Filter interface
Map the Filter to the endpoints that should be protected, through the @WebFilter annotation:

@WebFilter(urlPatterns = {"/country", "/capital"})
public class AuthenticationFilter implements Filter {
    // ...
}
Note that this mapping is an explicit list of protected URLs — unlike a /*-style pattern with exceptions, here only the endpoints listed in urlPatterns go through the Filter. Without the @WebFilter annotation, the Filter remains defined as a class, but is never executed by the server.


F.1.2 Step 3.2: Implement the Verification Logic in the Filter
The method to implement is doFilter(ServletRequest request, ServletResponse response, FilterChain chain). The parameters arrive with the generic type ServletRequest/ServletResponse — when auto-generated by Eclipse, they may appear with generic names like arg0, arg1, arg2; it is worth renaming them to something clearer. Before using them, they need to be cast to the HTTP types:
@Override
public void doFilter(ServletRequest request, ServletResponse response, FilterChain chain)
        throws IOException, ServletException {

    HttpServletRequest httpRequest = (HttpServletRequest) request;
    HttpServletResponse httpResponse = (HttpServletResponse) response;

    HttpSession session = httpRequest.getSession(false);

    if (session != null && session.getAttribute("UserName") != null) {
        chain.doFilter(request, response);
    } else {
        httpResponse.setStatus(HttpServletResponse.SC_UNAUTHORIZED);
        httpResponse.setContentType("application/json");
        PrintWriter out = httpResponse.getWriter();
        String jsonResponse = "{\"error\": \"Authentication required\"}";
        out.print(jsonResponse);
    }
}
The call to chain.doFilter(request, response) is what lets the request continue on to the target Servlet — if the session is not valid, that call should not be made, and the Filter responds directly with 401 UNAUTHORIZED.


F.1.3 Step 3.3: Remove the Manual Verification from the Servlet
With the Filter now handling authentication, the verification block added in Step 2.6 is no longer needed inside CountryServlet. Remove that block: the Servlet goes back to containing only its own endpoint-specific logic, with no access-control concerns.
This separation — business logic in the Servlet, access control in the Filter — makes the code cleaner and easier to maintain: to protect a new endpoint, it is enough to add its URL to urlPatterns in Step 3.1, with no need to touch any other code.


F.1.4 Step 3.4: Create the Logout Endpoint

Create a new Servlet called LogoutServlet, in the capitaisapi.servlets package, mapped to the /logout URL
Implement the doGet method:

HttpSession session = request.getSession(false);

if (session != null) {
    session.invalidate();
}

response.setContentType("application/json");
PrintWriter out = response.getWriter();
String jsonResponse = "{\"message\": \"Logout OK\"}";
out.print(jsonResponse);
session.invalidate() ends the session on the server side — from this point on, any later request carrying the same JSESSIONID (see Chapter 5) no longer finds a valid session.


F.1.5 Step 3.5: Integrated Testing

Access /country?countrycode=PT without logging in. Expected result: HTTP 401, {"error": "Authentication required"}
Log in through login.html (Appendix E) with javaee / asw2026. Expected result: HTTP 200 with the success response — and, at this point, confirm (for example, in the browser’s developer tools) that a JSESSIONID cookie was automatically created
Repeat the request to /country?countrycode=PT. Expected result: HTTP 200, with the country’s data
Access /logout. Expected result: HTTP 200, {"message": "Logout OK"}
Repeat the request to /country?countrycode=PT once more. Expected result: HTTP 401 again — logout invalidated the session



F.1.6 Conclusion
The API’s architecture is now more modular: authentication remains concentrated in the /login endpoint, but authorisation — deciding whether a given request may proceed — is no longer scattered across the Servlets, and is now centralised in a single Filter. In Appendix G, the fixed data used so far is replaced with real data, stored in a relational database.

